# Normalise line endings in the working tree, not in the object store.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# `.ai/` and the README milestone block are GENERATED, and
# tools/lego/ai-pack.mjs --check compares them by exact string equality against
# the generator's own output, which is always LF-terminated. Git for Windows
# defaults `core.autocrlf=true`, so a checkout on a Windows runner rewrites
# those LF endings to CRLF, and EVERY generated file then reads back as
# "out of date" -- together with the README milestone-governance block.
#
# That is not a projection drift. It is a platform artefact that makes a
# byte-reproducible generator unverifiable on any Windows runner.
#
# It stayed invisible because the n8n-lego architecture job never actually
# executed its steps in CI: the hosted jobs failed to start, so the gate never
# reached `ai-pack --check`. Once the job ran on the self-hosted Windows fleet
# (PR #416), 7 of the 8 gate failures were this one cause.
#
# `text=auto` keeps git's own text/binary detection, so binaries (PNGs, the
# offline tarball, lockfile-free assets) are untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The pinned n8n reference checkout is vendored verbatim and is never edited by
# this project. Normalising it would buy nothing and risks a mass
# renormalisation, so it keeps git's default behaviour.
reference/ -text
